      "raw_response": "SUPPORTED\n\nThe abstract supports the claim that assembly of invadopodia (podosomes) is triggered by focal generation of phosphatidylinositol-3,4-biphosphate (PtdIns(3,4)P2) and the activation of the nonreceptor tyrosine kinase Src. The abstract mentions that the podosomes in Src-transformed NIH3T3 cells are enriched with PtdIns(3,4)P2, suggesting an important role of this phosphoinositide in podosome formation. It also indicates that the expression of Src stimulates podosome formation at focal adhesions of NIH3T3 cells, which are associated with PtdIns(3,4)P2 accumulation. The study further supports the claim by describing the formation of a complex between Tks5/FISH, Grb2, and N-WASP at adhesion sites in an Src-dependent manner, which facilitates podosome formation. This aligns with the proposed mechanism of invadopodia formation in the claim.",
